course: dictionary
lesson: 9086
title: Slowly and how things happen
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Walk slowly.» Here slowly tells you how to walk: at a low speed, not fast. Slowly is an adverb, a word that tells how, when or where something happens. Many adverbs end in -ly.
explain: Slowly has a second meaning: over a long time, bit by bit. «The ice slowly melted.» The ice did not race; it changed over a long time. Read the words around slowly to see which meaning fits.
explain: Ask yourself: is this about speed, or about time? «Drive slowly near schools» is about speed. «Plants grow slowly» is about time. Then swap in your meaning and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Slowly is an adverb: it tells you how something happens.
fact: Slowly can mean at a low speed, not fast, as in «Walk slowly».
fact: Slowly can also mean over a long time, bit by bit, as in «The ice slowly melted».
fact: The comparative is more slowly, and the superlative is most slowly.
fact: Slowly but surely means getting there steadily, even if it takes a while.
exercise: «Walk slowly near the pool.» What does slowly mean here? | Without stopping | Very loudly | *At a low speed, not fast | Over a long time, bit by bit
exercise: «Rocks wear away slowly.» What does slowly tell us? | The rocks are shiny | *The change happens bit by bit over a long time | Rocks move very fast | The rocks are very loud
exercise: Which word in «Drive slowly near schools» is the adverb? | *slowly | drive | schools | near
test: «Breathe out slowly and hold a hand.» What does slowly mean here? | Very loudly | *At a low speed, not fast | Upside down | Suddenly, with a jump
test: What is the comparative form of slowly? | most slow | slowliest | *more slowly | Slowly more
exam: Which sentence uses slowly to mean over a long time? | *The ice slowly melted | Walk slowly on the wet floor | Drive slowly near the school | Breathe out slowly
